<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mukunda Deva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1559">1559</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mukunda Harichandana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mukunda Deva was the last independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, ruling from about 1559 to 1568. A minister of the Bhoi dynasty, he seized the throne by overthrowing its last rulers. In 1568 he was killed in a war with rebels while the Karrani sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> invaded, and Odisha passed under Afghan and later Mughal rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mukunda Deva, also called Mukunda Harichandana, traced his descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>, and the traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> call him Telinga Mukundadeva. His Bhimeswara temple inscription names his father as Saravaraju and his grandfather as Singaraju. He took the throne at Kataka in 1559 after killing Raghuram Raya Chotaraya, the last ruler of the Bhoi dynasty, and is counted as the founder and only monarch of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya dynasty</a> of Odisha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As king he tried to revive the power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. He laid out two streets from the Lion's Gateway to the Gundicha Temple and made the road smooth by filling its pits and holes. On the outer side of the southern walls he raised a swing arch, the Dola Mandap, on which the deities are swung during the Dola festival in the last five days of the month of Falguna. He also built a chain of forts at Raibania, in the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasore">Balasore</a> district.</p><p>His foreign relations were shaped by two powers: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, his enemy, and the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, his ally. In 1560 Sultan Ghiyasuddin Jalal Shah of Bengal invaded and marched as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajpur">Jajpur</a>, and Mukunda Deva defeated him and drove him out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1567, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> was occupied with the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a>, the Karrani sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> invaded. Mukunda Deva was defeated and took shelter in the fort of Kotsima, and the Bengal army under the general Kalapahada made a devastating attack on the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a>. In the king's absence Ramachandra Bhanja, a feudatory of Sarangagarh, declared himself king. Mukunda Deva hurried back and met him in battle at Gohiratikiri, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajpur">Jajpur</a>, where Mukunda Deva was killed.</p><p>Ramachandra Bhanja was himself later executed by the sultan's forces. Afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramachandra-deva-i">Ramachandra Deva I</a> of the Bhoi dynasty, son of Danai Vidyadhara, allied himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> to avoid an Afghan invasion and continued to rule as a subordinate king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mukunda Deva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1559 to 1560 the minister <b>Mukunda Deva</b> overthrew the last Bhoi rulers.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhoi-dynasty">Bhoi dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
